<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Seville</h1><p class="st-meta">1023 CE – 1091 CE · capital Seville</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most powerful taifa of al-Andalus, ruled by the Abbadids, whose poet-king al-Mutamid invited the Almoravids and lost his throne to them.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1023 the leading families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudid</a> garrison and entrusted the city to the qadi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-qasim-muhammad-ibn-abbad">Abu al-Qasim Muhammad ibn Abbad</a>, of an old Arab family. He ruled in the name of a shadowy pretender, supposedly the vanished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-ii">Hisham II</a>, which gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadids</a> a claim to wider authority.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid-of-seville">Al-Mutadid</a> (1042-1069) expanded ruthlessly, annexing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> of the south-west, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad">al-Mutamid</a> (1069-1091), himself a celebrated poet, added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in 1070 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> in 1078, with the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-ammar">Ibn Ammar</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, textiles and river port made it the wealthiest city in the west, though it paid tribute to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> fell in 1085, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad">al-Mutamid</a> called in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, who won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sagrajas">Sagrajas</a> in 1086 but deposed him in 1091. He died in exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghmat">Aghmat</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>, and his poems of loss became classics of Arabic literature.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-seville.jpg" alt="Taifa of Seville" width="320" height="159"><figcaption>Image: Tyk based on Image:Iberian Peninsula base map.svg created by Redtony · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reino_de_Sevilla_s._XI.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1023 CE – 1091 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Seville</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu al-Qasim Muhammad ibn Abbad, al-Mutadid, al-Mutamid</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Mozarabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Abbadid Kingdom of Seville</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1057&amp;focus=taifa-of-seville" aria-label="Taifa of Seville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4530 832 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Seville lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4850 1088L4863 1130M4850 1088L4854 1109M4850 1088L4859 1085M4850 1088L4881 1071M4850 1088L4826 1094M4850 1088L4809 1080M4850 1088L4863 1097M4850 1088L4972 1068M4850 1088L4833 1089M4850 1088L4870 1110M4850 1088L4828 927M4850 1088L4881 1071M4850 10